- Limits: 32 active VLANs, VLAN IDs 1–4000. The HNC VLAN set (16 VLANs, highest 997) fits.
- Spanning tree: the built-in switch runs RSTP/MSTP, and Palo Alto says neighbouring switches must not run PVST.
- HA: Palo Alto's design uses the same LAN SVI IPs on both units, a unique HA SVI IP, and interface tracking on the trunk port.
- Factory state: ports 5–10 are in access mode, and a vlan1 SVI exists but is admin-down.

What goes in the intent

In the device's interfaces array in sdwan_devices.json, add one SVI per VLAN and then the trunk port:

{
  "name": "vlan-100", "type": "vlan", "admin_up": true,
  "description": "VLAN 100 — Management",
  "scope": "global", "used_for": "lan",
  "vlan_config": { "vlan_id": 100, "voice_enabled": false, "mstp_instance": 0 },
  "ipv4_config": { "type": "static", "static_config": { "address": "10.97.7.1/27" } }
},
{
  "name": "vlan-20", "type": "vlan", "admin_up": true,
  "description": "SDW HA — control link",
  "scope": "local", "used_for": "ha",
  "vlan_config": { "vlan_id": 20, "voice_enabled": false, "mstp_instance": 0 },
  "ipv4_config": { "type": "static", "static_config": { "address": "10.159.134.209/30" } }
},
{
  "name": "7", "type": "switch_port", "admin_up": true,
  "description": "LAN trunk to access switch",
  "switch_port_config": {
    "vlan_mode": "trunk",
    "trunk_vlans": ["20", "100", "200"],
    "native_vlan_id": 100
  }
}

- VLAN references are plain numbers. trunk_vlans is a list of strings and native_vlan_id is a number; neither is an interface ID, so no new _ref_ resolver is needed.
- Port 7 needs only the fields you manage. The switch port always exists, so the SDK merges your keys onto the live object and keeps the rest (STP, storm control, used_for).
- HA follows the SVI name. spoke_ha_config.source_interface becomes "vlan-20", and tracking is { "_ref_interface_name": "7", "reduce_priority": <that device's priority> }.
- Nothing else moves. DHCP servers are keyed by subnet and zones resolve interfaces by name. The deploy repo has no intent schema that would reject the new types.

What the SDK review found

I ran the interface handler against a fake client modelled on the 1200-S factory defaults (no tenant calls). The trunk PUT and SVI POST went through, and a second run was a clean no-op. Two things need fixing:

1. Ordering (src/scm_auto/orchestrators/device_orchestrator.py:64): vlan and switch_port both fall in bucket 2, so the trunk is applied before its SVIs if the intent lists it first. Adding "switch_port": 3 to _INTERFACE_TYPE_BUCKET fixes it.
2. Delete (src/scm_auto/handlers/sdwan/devices/interfaces.py:222): remove() sends a DELETE for the switch port, which cannot be deleted. It needs a branch that PUTs the port back to access mode with its VLANs cleared, so the SVI deletes that follow are allowed.

docs/intent-format.md:695 would also need the two new types added.

Not yet confirmed against a live controller

- SVI create defaults: the SDK posts its generic defaults, including a populated multicast_config. That same field was rejected for bypass pairs, so the first SVI create may return a 400.
- trunk_vlans order: the SDK compares lists in exact order. If the controller re-orders them, every run will show a change on port 7.
- Names and strings: the exact SVI name the controller stores, the used_for value on a trunk port, and the model_name string for a 1200-S shell.
- LACP on switch ports: I found nothing in Palo Alto's docs on bundling two switch ports, so I can't say whether a two-port trunk is possible.

The WORC ION already runs this layout (port 7 trunk, native 100, SVIs). The interface JSON for its port 7 and one SVI, or a photo of it, would settle the first three points. With that I can write the two fixes and their tests against the real payload.

